## Context

Design v6 was adopted by PR #903. The former v4 train is superseded by the
[implementation plan](../docs/2026-09-11-memory-implementation-plan.md).
This tracker closes only after the core implementation union is reviewed.
Its dependencies deliberately keep it out of the ready queue until then.

The architecture is one internal Python library with thin CLI/adapters,
project-owned bodies plus a bounded shared snapshot, UUID lifecycle and
orientation/recent/task delivery. Rediscovery is only an opportunity for
generalisation; keep particulars unless explicitly withdrawn. Canonical
decisions stay in existing records. One ownership contract and optimistic Git
concurrency replace runtime modes and global locks.

## Relevant files

- `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design.md` — adopted v7, not edited by this plan
- `docs/2026-09-11-memory-implementation-plan.md` — exact ticket/dependency map
- Tickets 0908, 0910–0919 and 0920–0925 — implementation and optional experiments

## Actions

1. Foundation: 0911 and 0917; then legacy compatibility 0908 and admission 0915.
2. Pilot: 0920 → 0910 → 0921 → 0922 → 0923. First acceptance is one project,
   a small shared snapshot and one runtime in a disposable offline clone.
3. Expand through 0924 and 0918 → 0913, with measured readiness before broad activation/retirement. In parallel, develop
   manual editorial review through 0923 → 0916 and independent harvesting through 0923 → 0925. Base editorial workflows can use manual judgments; model-assisted automation is optional.
4. Keep 0912 (automated candidate discovery), 0914 (predicate extensions) and 0919 (quality annotation experiment)
   deferred and outside the core closure gate. Reactivate only on evidence.
5. Rerun the relevant 0918 behavioural scenarios on the completed union, adding accepted generalisation/local-exception and harvested-then-amended-procedure cases once 0916/0925 exist. Check their predeclared margins; early pilot results alone cannot certify these later features.
6. Review the merged union, actual adapter capabilities, migration coverage and
   evaluation results. Record any authorised scope changes; do not quietly omit
   a core child or pretend optional work was implemented.

## Test

The integration assessment must exercise active/inactive identity, generalisation
without source retirement, fixed available snapshots, G/C loading, recent writes,
lexical recall, canonical-owner routing, native-writer isolation and optimistic
conflicts. Behavioural judgments use repeated scenarios, not deterministic
semantic assertions. All substantive fixtures need a known failing control.

## Verification

- [ ] Every core child has a merged completion or an explicitly recorded scope decision.
- [ ] Applicable checks pass on the union; actual runtime smoke evidence is read.
- [ ] First-runtime evaluation and remaining-runtime capability declarations agree.
- [ ] No stale ticket demands mandatory residency, automatic promotion or a new ledger.

## Invariants

- This planning amendment closes no implementation work and writes no runtime code.
- Existing IDs and log entries remain; historical scope is available in Git.
- Decisions are not made by memory scores; shared semantic changes are reviewed.
- Optional experiments do not block a useful or complete baseline.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Foundation, portable pilot, all target adapters, rollout and editorial/harvesting
      work are integrated and reviewed together.
- [ ] Evaluation records behaviour and cost, including limitations; budget proposals
      are not silently promoted to policy.
- [ ] The implementation plan and v7 accurately describe the delivered contract, or
      any deviation is explicitly adopted through the normal decision process.
- [ ] Final integration review is recorded and only then this tracker is closed.
